@startuml class-diagram-sync-engine
title Class Diagram (Implementation) — Sync Engine
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor transparent
skinparam shadowing false
hide circle
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

class ConnectivityMonitor <<EventEmitter>> {
  -healthCheck : function
  -intervalMs : int
  -online : bool
  +start()
  +stop()
  +checkNow() : Promise
}

class SyncEngine <<factory>> {
  -monitor : ConnectivityMonitor
  -syncing : bool
  -lastResult : object
  +start()
  +stop()
  +runSyncPass() : Promise
  +getStatus() : object
}

class Outbox <<module>> {
  +enqueueSync(db, entityType, entityId, op, payload) : int
  +getPending(db, limit) : "Row[]"
  +markSynced(db, ids)
  +pendingCount(db) : int
}

class Push <<module>> {
  +pushPending(db, transport) : Promise
}

class Pull <<module>> {
  +pullRemote(db, transport, entityType) : Promise
}

class ConflictResolver <<module>> {
  +resolveConflict(entityType, localPayload, remotePayload) : object
}

class HttpTransport <<module>> {
  +health() : Promise
  +push(records) : Promise
  +pull(entityType, cursor) : Promise
}

SyncEngine *-- ConnectivityMonitor : owns
SyncEngine ..> Push : runs on 'checked' online
SyncEngine ..> Pull : runs on 'checked' online
Push ..> Outbox : reads pending, marks synced
Push ..> HttpTransport : POST /sync/push
Pull ..> HttpTransport : GET /sync/pull
Pull ..> ConflictResolver : resolves per entity

note "createSyncEngine({ db, transport, entityTypes, intervalMs }) is a factory\nfunction, not a class — it closes over db/transport/mutable state (syncing,\nlastResult) and returns a plain object exposing start/stop/runSyncPass/\ngetStatus/on. Modeled as SyncEngine <<factory>> because it plays the same\narchitectural role a class would, built with a closure instead of class/new." as N1
@enduml
